-    // for all in-flight callbacks to finish. We call close() here while node-level entities
-    // (subscriptions, publishers, etc.) are still alive — at shutdown time, the spin loop
-    // has already exited so there are no active callbacks, and wait_callbacks() returns
-    // immediately. The session is then marked as closed, so when the shared_ptr refcount
-    // eventually reaches zero (after rcl destroys each handle in the normal teardown order),
-    // the session destructor finds is_closed()==true and skips the blocking close() call.
-    session_->close();
+    // NOTE(iw): Intentionally leak a reference to the zenoh Session rather than
+    // calling close(). zenoh-c's z_close() drives the tokio runtime; when this
+    // shutdown() runs during process-exit static destruction (the rclcpp Context
+    // is torn down from __cxa_finalize, e.g. when a program does not call
+    // rclcpp::shutdown() explicitly), the runtime's thread-local state is already
+    // gone and z_close() panics with a TLS AccessError. Because z_close() is an
+    // extern "C" boundary that "cannot unwind", that panic aborts the process.
+    // Holding an extra Session reference that is never released ensures the
+    // Session destructor (which would otherwise call close()) never runs, so
+    // z_close() is never invoked during teardown. The OS reclaims the underlying
+    // transport at exit, and the router detects the departed node via liveliness.
+    new std::shared_ptr<zenoh::Session>(session_);
 
     session_.reset();
 
